    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey so im studying for my security+ exam and i'm a bit confused. i get that hashing turns a password into a string of characters, but why do we actually need to add a 'salt' to it? like if the hash is already unique, why does the extra step matter?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "A salt is unique to each user or password, but it does not inherently create uniqueness because an attacker can generate a massive number of possible salts themselves to brute-force each real salt. The real benefit is making a dictionary attack incredibly expensive since every salted attempt requires a full hash computation rather than just a table lookup, while also preventing databases with similar password databases from having their hashes pre-computed by attackers. Without salts, an attacker only needs to break one password hash and then use that same solution against thousands of other users who likely chose similar weak passwords.",
