    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "okay i think i get the part about dictionary attacks. but i was reading about rainbow tables specifically. how exactly does the salt stop a rainbow table from working? like if the attacker already has a huge list of precomputed hashes, doesnt the salt just become another thing they have to account for? wouldn't they just make rainbow tables for common salts too?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Rainbow tables are precomputed databases of hashes for *un-salted* passwords, which is why salts effectively force an attacker to discard those saved dictionaries entirely. While existing salts theoretically exist, unique salts ensure that for every user, the attacker cannot simply look up a match in a pre-made table; they must instead compute a fresh hash for that combined input from scratch. Consequently, investing resources to build a pre-computed list of salts is computationally impractical and is treated as an equally broken avenue for storing rainbow tables.",
