    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "that makes sense. so the salt basically makes the \"pre-computed\" part of the rainbow table useless because the table would have to be built for every single possible salt value. is there any other way an attacker could try to bypass this, or is salting pretty much the gold standard for protecting stored passwords from those kinds of mass-lookup attacks?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "While salting is the gold standard for preventing mass-lookup attacks via rainbow tables, attackers are increasingly targeting the *source* of your passwords (like phishing) rather than trying to crack stored hashes in bulk. Additionally, salting only protects data silently; obtaining a clear text password dump via social engineering or exploiting exposed features still bypasses encryption entirely. Therefore, combining unique salts with strong unique Keys per site is usually the most robust defense against modern, sophisticated threats.",
      "source": "target",
